    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "ok i get that part. so like, if two people have the same password, say \"Password123\", they'll have the exact same hash if we don't use a salt? and how exactly does adding that random string stop someone from just using a rainbow table for the salt too?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "If two people use the same password, like \"Password123\", the resulting hashes would be the same. Salting ensures that even if two people use the same password, the resulting hashes will be different, making it harder to use a precomputed table of hashes to look up the password.\n\nTo address your second question, a rainbow table wouldn't be effective for salted hashes, since the salt value is not typically stored with the hash. Instead, the salt value is usually stored alongside the hashed password, and it's included in the final password verification process. This way, an attacker would need to compute the rainbow table with the same salt value used for the stored hash, rather than just looking up the plain password hash.",
      "source": "target",
